// Per-lane writes from a COMPUTED vector must each read their own lane
// (t_856689b2, the last four general-path mismatches).
//
// The general path's VecInsert lowering forced the scalar source's swizzle
// to lane 0.  That is right for a literal - lane 0 of the const block - and
// wrong for a LANE EXTRACT, whose lane resolve() had already selected.  So
//
//     color.y = lit.y;
//
// emitted `MOV R0.y, R16.x`: the red channel broadcast into green and blue.
// The rig saw it as our R level count matching the reference's while G and
// B were copies of R, on four shaders whose reference paints three distinct
// channels.  broadcastScalar's own comment warns against forcing lane 0 for
// exactly this reason.
void main(float4 v : TEXCOORD0,
          out float4 oColor : COLOR)
{
    float4 lit = v * float4(0.5f, 0.25f, 0.125f, 1.0f);
    float4 c = v;
    c.x = lit.x;
    c.y = lit.y;
    c.z = lit.z;
    oColor = c;
}
